Photosynthesis Photosynthesis is the process by which plants, algae, and some bacteria convert light energy into chemical energy. 
In plants, photosynthesis primarily occurs in chloroplasts. Chloroplasts contain a green pigment called chlorophyll that absorbs light energy. 
Photosynthesis uses carbon dioxide and water as reactants. Light energy provides the energy needed to produce glucose and oxygen. 
The overall process can be summarized as carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, produces glucose and oxygen. 
Photosynthesis occurs in two major stages: the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle. 
The light-dependent reactions occur in the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts. 
Light energy is used to produce ATP and NADPH. Water is split during these reactions, releasing oxygen as a byproduct. 
The Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma of the chloroplast. It uses ATP and NADPH to help convert carbon dioxide into carbohydrates. 
Glucose produced through photosynthesis can be used as an energy source or stored for later use. 
Photosynthesis is important because it converts solar energy into chemical energy and produces much of the oxygen used by aerobic organisms.
The rate of photosynthesis can be affected by factors such as light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration, temperature, and water availability
